<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/tree-down-street.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/tree-down-street-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/tree-down-street.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/cities-we-serve/">Cities</a></li><li aria-current="page">Everett</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">North Puget Sound</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser for Everett, WA</h1><p class="lede">Everett and the rest of Snohomish County sit where Puget Sound weather meets the Cascade foothills. Windstorms, falling trees and winter freezes drive most claims here.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Snohomish County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>North Puget Sound</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="note local"><b class="h">Local claim risks in Everett</b><ul class="chips"><li>Windstorms</li><li>Falling trees</li><li>Water leaks</li><li>Snow &amp; ice</li><li>River flooding (flood policy)</li></ul><p class="housing"><b>Housing mix:</b> Older north Everett homes, post-war ranches, newer subdivisions and growing condo stock.</p></div>

<h2>Snohomish County weather</h2>
<p>Everett is the seat of Snohomish County. Its waterfront, port and aerospace employers anchor a county that stretches from Puget Sound into the Cascades. Winter windstorms are the biggest regular threat. The Hanukkah Eve Storm in 2006 and the November 2024 bomb cyclone both caused widespread tree damage and outages across the county.</p>
<p>The Snohomish, Stillaguamish and Skykomish rivers flood in heavy winter rains, and the 2014 Oso landslide showed the county’s geologic risks. Flood and earth movement are excluded from standard homeowner policies, so separate coverage matters here.</p>
<h2>Housing in and around Everett</h2>
<p>North Everett has older homes, some dating to the early 1900s. South Everett, Mill Creek, Lynnwood and Marysville have large tracts of post-war and newer homes. Condominium and apartment construction has grown along the transit corridors.</p>
<h2>Common Everett claims</h2>
<ul>
<li>Falling trees on roofs and garages during windstorms.</li>
<li>Roof and siding damage on exposed hillsides above the Sound.</li>
<li>Burst pipes and water damage during cold snaps.</li>
<li>Condominium water losses that affect more than one unit.</li>
</ul>

